DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'MathObjects.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');
TEXT(beginproblem());
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
$prompt = <<'PROMPT_C164_FAAA';
<p>c164_faaa</p> <table border="0" style="border: 0px solid white; border-collapse: collapse; "><colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <colgroup width="60"></colgroup> <tr><td rowspan="2"></td><td align="center">Gene 1</td><td rowspan="2"></td></tr><tr> <td align="center"><span style="color: red;"><b>&#10005;</b></span>&darr;<span style="color: red;"><b>&#10005;</b></span></td> <td align="center">Gene 2</td></tr><tr> <td align="center" style="background-color: ivory">white</td> <td align="center">&xrarr;</td> <td align="center" style="background-color: cornflowerblue">blue</td> <td align="center">&xrarr;</td> <td align="center" style="background-color: powderblue">sky blue</td></tr></table><br/> A two step metabolic pathway determines the pigment color.<li> <ul>When gene 1 is dominant, the gene expresses an enzyme that inhibits another enzyme that converts the white precursor pigment into the blue pigment.</ul> <ul>When gene 2 is dominant, the gene expresses an enzyme that converts the blue pigment into the sky blue pigment.</ul> <ul>When both genes are dominant, the dominant allele of gene 1 inhibits the production of the blue color, so it will remain the white precursor color.</ul> <ul>When both of the genes are homozygous recessive, then the first enzyme is active (not inhibited) and the white precursor pigment is converted to the blue pigment and it will remain the final pigment, as the second enzyme is inactive.</ul></li> <br/> <br/><p>The diagram and description above explain the interaction of two genes. <b>Determine the dihybird cross phenotypic ratio.</b></p>
PROMPT_C164_FAAA
loadMacros('parserRadioButtons.pl');
$choice_1 = <<'LITERAL_C164_FAAA_CHOICE_1';
